WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:03.211
By the time I was 6
months post diagnosis,

00:00:03.211 --> 00:00:05.672
 I was taking 42 pills a day.

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:07.674
I have so many 
different medications

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:08.883
that we sometimes joke

00:00:08.883 --> 00:00:13.096
that Covid is afraid of me.

00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:17.308
ALS is characterized 
as a progressive

00:00:17.308 --> 00:00:18.685
neurological disorder,

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:20.812
meaning your nerves 
break down over time,

00:00:20.812 --> 00:00:23.148
resulting in progressive paralysis.

00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:26.568
In my family, it typically starts
as weakness, usually in the leg.

00:00:26.568 --> 00:00:29.195
My voice is going

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:32.157
and I can barely feed myself.

00:00:32.157 --> 00:00:35.535
It's like, do you want 
to go to hell fast?

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:37.037
Or do you want 
to go to hell slow?

00:00:37.412 --> 00:00:38.955
There is no cure.

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:40.331
There is no effective treatment.

00:00:40.331 --> 00:00:43.209
It's one of the most terrifying
and hardest to treat diseases

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:44.002
that are out there.

00:00:44.002 --> 00:00:45.837
This disease is different.

00:00:45.837 --> 00:00:47.338
We have to think 
about it differently

00:00:47.338 --> 00:00:48.506
and we have to use our flexibility

00:00:48.506 --> 00:00:51.301
to think about how 
we approve drugs

00:00:51.301 --> 00:00:54.429
 for a disease that is 100% fatal.

00:01:01.144 --> 00:01:04.272
I am ready to accept the 
ALS Ice Bucket Challenge.

00:01:04.272 --> 00:01:05.982
Where's the water? 
Where's the ice?

00:01:05.982 --> 00:01:07.400
Alright.

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:08.818
Whoa.

00:01:08.818 --> 00:01:11.404
The Ice Bucket Challenge in 2014

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:12.739
drew a lot of attention

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:14.240
to this horrible disease

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.493
and brought millions 
of new funding

00:01:16.493 --> 00:01:17.702
into disease research.

00:01:17.702 --> 00:01:20.080
Because patients die so 
quickly from the disease,

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.708
ALS historically hasn't generated
the same level of public attention

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:25.335
as conditions like breast cancer, 

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:27.879
which has a large 
constituency of survivors.

00:01:27.879 --> 00:01:31.883
ALS stands for amyotrophic
lateral sclerosis,

00:01:31.883 --> 00:01:34.886
a fatal and progressive
neurodegenerative disease

00:01:34.886 --> 00:01:37.055
in which the motor neurons

00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:38.807
that control the 
voluntary muscles

00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:40.809
slowly and progressively die.

00:01:40.809 --> 00:01:42.310
And the types of 
muscles that are affected

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.229
in ALS, they include 
everything down,

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:46.564
not just the muscles
in our arms and legs,

00:01:46.564 --> 00:01:48.566
but the muscles 
responsible for breathing

00:01:48.566 --> 00:01:51.152
and the muscles responsible
for eating and swallowing,

00:01:51.152 --> 00:01:52.570
which means patients,

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.823
they literally are 
alive and fully conscious

00:01:54.823 --> 00:01:56.074
but can't move at all.

00:01:56.074 --> 00:01:59.661
The causes of most forms 
of ALS are not known.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.331
However, there are about 10%
of the cases that are familial.

00:02:03.331 --> 00:02:04.874
 And in recent years,

00:02:04.874 --> 00:02:06.334
scientists have 
started to discover

00:02:06.334 --> 00:02:10.505
some of the genes responsible
for some of these familial cases.

00:02:10.505 --> 00:02:13.967
Another 90% of cases are 
so-called sporadic cases,

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:15.844
which means that 
researchers really have no idea

00:02:15.844 --> 00:02:18.012
about what causes those cases.

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:23.434
I think this document’s 
really priceless.

00:02:23.434 --> 00:02:26.146
It shows just how 
many generations

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:28.815
and how many families 
have been impacted.

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:31.442
My family carries a 
gene called SOD1

00:02:31.442 --> 00:02:33.486
and there’s only 
about 300 people

00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:35.947
in this country that 
carry that particular gene.

00:02:35.947 --> 00:02:40.451
My grandfather, 
Harold John Lindsey, died of ALS.

00:02:40.451 --> 00:02:42.495
His brother, 
Wesley Lindsey, also died.

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:43.413
And he was quite young.

00:02:43.413 --> 00:02:44.497
He was only 30.

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:47.250
We believe my mom is the 
33rd person to have gotten

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.419
 ALS in our family.

00:02:49.419 --> 00:02:51.171
After my uncle was diagnosed,

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:53.673
he asked his siblings 
to please get tested.

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:56.593
My mother's 2 other 
siblings tested negative

00:02:56.593 --> 00:02:59.596
and then my 
mother tested positive.

00:02:59.596 --> 00:03:01.598
Her first symptom was that 
she couldn't lift her leg up

00:03:01.598 --> 00:03:03.600
off the floor
to get into bed.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.560
Within a few months, 
it became apparent

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.520
that she wasn't walking very well.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.232
She called me and she said, 
honey, I need to see someone.

00:03:12.609 --> 00:03:13.484
I was at work.

00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:15.945
And I walked out 
to the parking lot

00:03:15.945 --> 00:03:17.614
and I screamed.

00:03:19.741 --> 00:03:21.826
Because I knew 
what was coming.

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:26.122
The first thing I did was try and get her into the same trial

00:03:26.122 --> 00:03:27.207
that my uncle had been in

00:03:27.207 --> 00:03:29.500
because I knew that 
drug would work for her.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.960
A drug called tofersen

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:32.629
through a company 
called Biogen.

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:34.631
It was for our specific mutation

00:03:34.631 --> 00:03:36.758
and it was the best 
option out there.

00:03:36.758 --> 00:03:38.468
The Biogen drug tofersen is a

00:03:38.468 --> 00:03:39.928
very interesting

00:03:39.928 --> 00:03:41.262
new approach to ALS.

00:03:41.262 --> 00:03:42.305
It is potentially

00:03:42.305 --> 00:03:44.098
the first drug

00:03:44.098 --> 00:03:46.976
that would target a known 
genetic cause of ALS.

00:03:47.518 --> 00:03:49.938
Biogen is trying to 
get tofersen approved

00:03:49.938 --> 00:03:51.564
based on the fact that it

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:54.359
lowers blood levels of a protein

00:03:54.359 --> 00:03:55.777
called neurofilament.

00:03:55.777 --> 00:03:57.362
A neurofilament is something 

00:03:57.362 --> 00:03:58.404
that is produced

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:00.448
when brain or nerve cells die.

00:04:00.448 --> 00:04:01.574
And Biogen is saying 

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:03.368
that tofersen lowers levels 

00:04:03.368 --> 00:04:04.911
of neurofilament and that is

00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:08.289
an indicator that it's likely
to slow progression of ALS

00:04:08.289 --> 00:04:10.458
and is doing something good
in terms of protecting

00:04:10.458 --> 00:04:13.044
the motor neurons 
in the spinal cord.

00:04:13.044 --> 00:04:15.046
However, in the phase 3 
trial of Biogen’s drug,

00:04:15.046 --> 00:04:17.423
tofersen failed to 
meet its primary goal

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:18.841
of slowing decline.

00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:22.011
But they are seeing 
promising effects and

00:04:22.011 --> 00:04:25.473
this drug could set a precedent
for other types of drugs

00:04:25.473 --> 00:04:28.810
against specific genetic 
causes of ALS in the future.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:32.563
And if we unlock cures for ALS,
we will also unlock cures

00:04:32.563 --> 00:04:35.608
for Parkinson's, 
Alzheimer's and beyond.

00:04:38.528 --> 00:04:40.363
I was diagnosed with ALS

00:04:40.363 --> 00:04:43.241
about 5 years ago
when I was 37,

00:04:43.241 --> 00:04:44.325
and as you can see,

00:04:44.325 --> 00:04:47.620
Sandra is my voice.

00:04:49.872 --> 00:04:51.374
The majority of ALS patients

00:04:51.374 --> 00:04:53.334
have no access to 
promising treatments.

00:04:53.334 --> 00:04:57.088
I have not been able to 
qualify for a clinical trial

00:04:57.088 --> 00:04:58.172
for over 2 years,

00:04:58.172 --> 00:05:00.300
and so what that means,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.552
I'm left to cobble 
together medicine.

00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:04.304
I'm left to wander on my own.

00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:06.514
And that does not feel right.

00:05:06.514 --> 00:05:07.890
What we are asking for

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:11.019
is not for the FDA to lower 
the bar for efficacy

00:05:11.019 --> 00:05:14.856
but instead to add to 
their calculation 2 things.

00:05:14.856 --> 00:05:17.150
Number 1, the serious
nature of ALS.

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:20.320
And number 2, the lack 
of meaningful treatments.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.696
What I need you to do

00:05:21.696 --> 00:05:23.239
is to approve treatments

00:05:23.239 --> 00:05:25.908
that are safe and 
may extend my life.

00:05:25.908 --> 00:05:27.660
Give me the ability to choose

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:31.706
whether I try that treatment

00:05:31.706 --> 00:05:37.045
or I leave my fate to ALS.

00:05:41.049 --> 00:05:42.592
There are only 3 main drugs

00:05:42.592 --> 00:05:46.471
approved for ALS and they all have very modest effects.

00:05:46.471 --> 00:05:49.515
The first drug called 
Rilutek or Riluzole

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:52.060
was approved in 1995 in the US.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:54.771
The second drug was 
approved in the US in 2017

00:05:54.771 --> 00:05:56.105
from a Japanese drug company.

00:05:56.105 --> 00:05:57.398
It's called Radicava.

00:05:57.398 --> 00:05:58.649
And then just this year,

00:05:58.649 --> 00:06:01.027
the third main ALS 
drug was approved.

00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:02.779
It’s from Amylyx Pharmaceuticals.

00:06:02.779 --> 00:06:05.073
At least 40 drugs 
are now in trials,

00:06:05.073 --> 00:06:07.116
according to the 
ALS Association.

00:06:07.116 --> 00:06:09.952
Drug development is a 
long and arduous process.

00:06:09.952 --> 00:06:12.747
It can take decades
between when researchers

00:06:12.747 --> 00:06:15.666
first have an idea for a drug
to when it gets on the market.

00:06:15.666 --> 00:06:18.544
Historically, the US Food and 
Drug Administration has demanded that

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:21.714
demanded that drugs be proven both safe and effective

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:22.924
before they're approved for use.

00:06:22.924 --> 00:06:26.427
Effective in the past has 
meant that a drug basically

00:06:26.427 --> 00:06:28.221
directly impact the symptoms

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:31.349
and/or effect a disease 
has on patients’ lives.

00:06:31.349 --> 00:06:34.644
But in recent years, the FDA has 
often been approving drugs

00:06:34.644 --> 00:06:36.354
based on lab tests that indicate

00:06:36.354 --> 00:06:39.524
a drug is likely to improve
symptoms or extend life

00:06:39.524 --> 00:06:42.652
or otherwise impact the
clinical course of the disease.

00:06:42.652 --> 00:06:44.445
The accelerated approvals
have been most common for cancer drugs people

00:06:44.445 --> 00:06:46.072
cancer drugs.

00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:49.575
People advocating for
reform of accelerated approval

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:52.537
worry that if you just 
approve many, many drugs

00:06:52.537 --> 00:06:55.164
and we don't prove 
that they're effective,

00:06:55.164 --> 00:06:56.958
you could have many drugs
that are on the market

00:06:56.958 --> 00:06:58.501
that aren't really effective.

00:06:58.501 --> 00:07:01.504
And all you're left 
with is risk and cost.

00:07:05.633 --> 00:07:08.177
Something that you'll hear
frequently in the ALS community

00:07:08.177 --> 00:07:11.597
is ALS is 100% fatal.

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:13.724
So we have a very high 
tolerance for risk.

00:07:13.724 --> 00:07:16.727
If there is a chance it could 
help us, we are willing to take it.

00:07:18.146 --> 00:07:21.399
July of 2021, my mom started 
tofersen expanded access.

00:07:21.399 --> 00:07:25.111
She immediately noticed 
a halt in her symptoms.

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:27.113
Things were really good
until about Christmas.

00:07:27.113 --> 00:07:29.657
She didn't have hardly
any progression at all.

00:07:29.657 --> 00:07:31.701
And then by January she 
started to progress more.

00:07:31.701 --> 00:07:35.830
In April, she developed blood 
clots in both lungs, so she

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:40.001
 actually had that next tofersen
injection delayed by a month.

00:07:40.001 --> 00:07:41.794
In May, my mother 
moved in with my sister

00:07:41.794 --> 00:07:43.004
under hospice’s care

00:07:43.004 --> 00:07:45.047
and she decided to 
stop all treatments.

00:07:45.047 --> 00:07:46.966
She was tired.

00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:49.552
It was just really hard on her.

00:07:49.552 --> 00:07:52.930
August, things got 
very progressive

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:55.558
and she passed away 
on August 30th.

00:07:55.558 --> 00:07:56.851
I think as soon as someone

00:07:56.851 --> 00:07:58.978
in your family or 
someone you love

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:01.022
gets an ALS diagnosis 

00:08:01.022 --> 00:08:03.483
and you see the fight 
that we’re up against,

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:06.819
you see the limited
options, the lack of hope.

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:09.280
It kind of turns everyone 
into a warrior.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:10.531
And we all just want to fight.

00:08:10.531 --> 00:08:13.618
Fight for our loved ones, 
fight for ourselves.

00:08:13.618 --> 00:08:17.163
Together, you and I, we will 
end this legacy of death.

00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:19.749
We will continue on 
with a legacy of hope.

00:08:20.374 --> 00:08:23.669
I came up with the idea 
for I AM ALS in 2018

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:24.879
to put together a nonprofit

00:08:24.879 --> 00:08:26.631
that was able to help ALS 
patients and their families

00:08:26.631 --> 00:08:28.966
navigate the disease.

00:08:28.966 --> 00:08:31.302
And we show them the 
power of their voice

00:08:31.302 --> 00:08:33.012
and help them tell their story.

00:08:33.012 --> 00:08:36.557
For the last 3 years,
we have been able to increase

00:08:36.557 --> 00:08:39.560
federal funding for ALS research,
and we have been able

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:40.728
to pass 2 laws

00:08:40.728 --> 00:08:43.397
and we have been able 
to work with BioPharma

00:08:43.397 --> 00:08:45.358
and the FDA and NIH

00:08:45.358 --> 00:08:47.068
to rethink how we approach

00:08:47.068 --> 00:08:51.072
the development of treatments 
for ALS patients.

00:08:51.072 --> 00:08:54.700
When we launched I AM ALS,
I told Sandra that my goal

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:57.703
was to increase money 
for ALS research

00:08:57.703 --> 00:09:00.748
by $100 million in 
our first 3 years.

00:09:00.748 --> 00:09:02.375
And I am happy to say

00:09:02.375 --> 00:09:07.255
that we have increased it 
by over $600 million.

00:09:11.342 --> 00:09:14.220
When my mom was first 
diagnosed, she said, honey,

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.305
I don't want ALS to be your life.

00:09:17.807 --> 00:09:19.141
But ALS is my life.

00:09:20.268 --> 00:09:22.603
I had a 50/50 shot of 
carrying the gene.

00:09:22.603 --> 00:09:24.855
I realized that I needed 
to be tested as well.

00:09:24.855 --> 00:09:28.067
I knew as soon as I 
saw the geneticist’s face

00:09:28.067 --> 00:09:29.527
that my results were positive.

00:09:29.527 --> 00:09:32.280
And when she said it 
out loud, I collapsed.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:34.365
I just collapsed in thinking,

00:09:36.826 --> 00:09:38.327
what about my children?

00:09:39.328 --> 00:09:42.915
Each of my children have a 
50/50 shot of carrying this gene.

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:46.627
But as a parent, as a mom,
to think that you may have

00:09:46.627 --> 00:09:50.256
passed something like this onto
your children is heartbreaking.

00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:51.757
But when I was able 
to pick myself up

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:53.676
and look at that screen again,

00:09:53.676 --> 00:09:57.096
the first words out of  the
neurologist’s mouth were,

00:09:57.096 --> 00:09:59.348
your children will not
have to worry about this.

00:10:02.893 --> 00:10:04.020
I am filled with hope

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:06.772
that my children will live 
in a world without ALS.

00:10:07.773 --> 00:10:10.443
Numerous drug companies 
are pursuing ALS drugs

00:10:10.443 --> 00:10:13.029
and certainly the standards
the FDA develops

00:10:13.029 --> 00:10:16.198
for approving ALS 
drugs could influence

00:10:16.198 --> 00:10:17.867
numerous drug applications

00:10:17.867 --> 00:10:19.619
that come forward 
for ALS in the future.

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:21.579
So I may be an optimist,

00:10:21.579 --> 00:10:23.289
but I think we are only 
a few years away

00:10:23.289 --> 00:10:24.999
where a doctor can 
say to a patient,

00:10:24.999 --> 00:10:27.084
you have ALS, but 
the good news is

00:10:27.084 --> 00:10:29.629
I have treatments that 
we can try for you.

00:10:29.629 --> 00:10:32.340
Well it’s like that starfish story.

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:34.675
The guy was walking 
on the beach and

00:10:34.675 --> 00:10:36.010
he could only throw a couple in,

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:38.137
but they appreciated it

00:10:39.388 --> 00:10:41.182
because they were saved.

00:10:42.016 --> 00:10:43.351
Thank you again.

00:10:43.351 --> 00:10:44.268
Thank you so much.

00:10:44.268 --> 00:10:45.186
Bye.

